    "content": "<!-- wp:cil/page-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Condition · Inflammation of the glans\",\"title\":\"Recurrent Balanitis and Circumcision in London\",\"lede\":\"Balanitis is inflammation of the head of the penis (glans). When the foreskin is also inflamed, this is sometimes called balanoposthitis.\",\"reviewedBy\":\"\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/team/#haidar\\u0022\\u003eDr Haidar Al-Ali, BDS, MFDS RCPS (Glasg)\\u003c/a\\u003e, September 2026\",\"crumbs\":[{\"label\":\"Reasons\",\"href\":\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\"},{\"label\":\"Balanitis\",\"href\":\"\"}]} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section-sm\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section-sm\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Symptoms can include redness, soreness, itching, swelling, discharge, an unpleasant smell and discomfort around the head of the penis or foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A single episode of balanitis does not necessarily mean that circumcision is required unless you are doing a circumcision for religious/ cultural or personal reasons. However, some men and boys experience recurrent balanitis, where inflammation repeatedly returns.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When recurrent balanitis is associated with a tight, difficult-to-retract or scarred foreskin, circumcision may provide a definitive solution by permanently removing the foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>At Beverley Clinic in North-West London, we assess and circumcise children, teenagers and adults with recurrent balanitis and associated foreskin problems.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/spec-panel {\"eyebrow\":\"At a glance\",\"rows\":[{\"k\":\"Who\",\"v\":\"Children, teenagers and adults\"},{\"k\":\"Factors\",\"v\":\"Tight foreskin, phimosis, BXO, diabetes\"},{\"k\":\"Consultation\",\"v\":\"Clinical assessment before procedure\"},{\"k\":\"Anaesthetic\",\"v\":\"Local anaesthetic for adults\"},{\"k\":\"Technique\",\"v\":\"Forceps-guided with thermal cautery\"},{\"k\":\"Adult price\",\"v\":\"Medical / foreskin problem: £1,080\"}],\"note\":\"Adult circumcision with a medical or foreskin problem is £1,080. Children assessed individually. See our \\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/prices/\\u0022 style=\\u0022color:var(\\u002d\\u002dblue-deep)\\u0022\\u003eprice list\\u003c/a\\u003e.\"} /--></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"symptoms\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"symptoms\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Symptoms \\u0026 pattern\",\"heading\":\"What are the symptoms of balanitis?\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Symptoms can include:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Redness or inflammation of the glans</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Swelling</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Soreness or tenderness</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Itching or irritation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pain or discomfort</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Discharge underneath the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>An unpleasant smell</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Difficulty retracting the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pain when passing urine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bleeding or cracking around the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Increased foreskin tightness</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Repeated episodes of inflammation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The appearance can vary according to the underlying cause and the patient's skin tone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you repeatedly experience these symptoms, particularly together with increasing foreskin tightness, an assessment is important.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Recurring cycle\",\"title\":\"What is recurrent balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eRecurrent balanitis means that episodes of inflammation repeatedly return after apparently improving.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA patient may experience redness and soreness, recover, and then develop another episode weeks or months later.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor some patients this becomes a frustrating cycle.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eRepeated inflammation can also occur together with phimosis, where the foreskin becomes increasingly difficult to retract.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen recurrent balanitis is associated with a troublesome foreskin, circumcision may be considered as a definitive surgical treatment.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Underlying factors\",\"title\":\"Why does balanitis keep coming back?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThere is not one cause of recurrent balanitis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFactors can include:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eA tight or difficult-to-retract foreskin\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDifficulty cleaning underneath the foreskin\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eRepeated irritation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eInfection\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eMoisture trapped beneath the foreskin\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003ePhimosis\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eForeskin scarring\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eLichen sclerosis / BXO\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDiabetes or poorly controlled blood glucose\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eOther inflammatory or dermatological conditions\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eSometimes several factors are present at the same time.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis is why somebody experiencing repeated episodes should not assume that every recurrence has exactly the same cause.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"foreskin-complications\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"foreskin-complications\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Foreskin complications\",\"heading\":\"Tight foreskin, phimosis and lichen sclerosis\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Vicious cycle\",\"title\":\"What is the connection between balanitis and a tight foreskin?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eBalanitis and phimosis commonly occur together.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf the foreskin is tight and difficult to retract, it can be more difficult to expose and clean the glans.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eAt the same time, repeated inflammation can contribute to changes in the foreskin, making it less elastic and more difficult to retract.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eThis can create a cycle:\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eTight foreskin → difficulty retracting and cleaning → inflammation → further tightness or scarring → recurrent inflammation\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen this occurs repeatedly, treating the underlying foreskin problem becomes an important consideration.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about phimosis →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Progressive scarring\",\"title\":\"Can balanitis cause phimosis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eRepeated or significant inflammation can contribute to scarring and tightening of the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA patient who could previously retract normally may notice that the foreskin becomes progressively tighter following repeated episodes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThere may eventually be a distinct tight ring, cracking or difficulty retracting over the glans.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf this happens, we assess both the recurrent balanitis and the resulting foreskin tightness when planning circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Hygiene \\u0026 tightness\",\"title\":\"Can phimosis cause balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eYes, the relationship can also work in the other direction.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA significantly tight foreskin can make it difficult to expose the glans and clean underneath the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSome patients with phimosis therefore experience repeated inflammation as well as tightness.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhere recurrent balanitis and phimosis occur together, circumcision removes the problematic foreskin as well as the tight foreskin ring.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about phimosis and tight foreskin →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Chronic condition\",\"title\":\"Balanitis and lichen sclerosis / BXO\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eLichen sclerosis, also known as BXO (balanitis xerotica obliterans) when affecting the penis, can cause chronic inflammation and scarring involving the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe foreskin may become:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhite or pale\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThickened\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eScarred\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eLess elastic\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDifficult to retract\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eProne to cracking or splitting\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eThe opening of the foreskin can progressively narrow and produce phimosis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf recurrent inflammation is accompanied by whitening or significant scarring of the foreskin, it is important that this is recognised during assessment because it can affect how the circumcision should be planned.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/bxo/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about lichen sclerosis / BXO →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"health-and-lifestyle\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"health-and-lifestyle\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Health \\u0026 lifestyle\",\"heading\":\"Diabetes, infections, sexual activity and symptoms\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Medical health\",\"title\":\"Balanitis and diabetes\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eRecurrent balanitis can sometimes be associated with diabetes, particularly where blood glucose is poorly controlled.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis is particularly relevant when an adult develops repeated episodes without an obvious previous history.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you have recurrent balanitis and symptoms or risk factors suggesting diabetes, you may need appropriate medical assessment through your GP.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision can address the foreskin component of recurrent balanitis, but it does not replace appropriate investigation or management of an underlying medical condition.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Sexual health\",\"title\":\"Is balanitis a sexually transmitted infection?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eBalanitis itself is not one specific sexually transmitted infection.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, some infections and sexually transmitted conditions can cause symptoms affecting the penis that may resemble balanitis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you have urethral discharge, genital sores, ulcers, unusual lesions or concerns following sexual contact, appropriate assessment through a GP or sexual health service may be required.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eDo not assume that every red or sore area on the penis is caused by the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Intimacy\",\"title\":\"Can balanitis affect sex?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eDuring an active episode, the glans and foreskin may be sore, inflamed or swollen, making sexual activity uncomfortable.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf recurrent balanitis is accompanied by phimosis, the foreskin may also become painful when stretched during an erection or intercourse.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSome patients experience cracking or bleeding around a tight foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhen considering circumcision, we assess whether the problem is recurrent inflammation alone or recurrent inflammation combined with tightness, scarring or another foreskin condition.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Discharge \\u0026 odour\",\"title\":\"Can balanitis cause a bad smell or discharge?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eYes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eInflammation underneath the foreskin can sometimes be associated with discharge and an unpleasant smell.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, discharge can have different causes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf discharge appears to be coming from the urethra itself rather than from underneath the foreskin, or there is a possibility of a sexually transmitted infection, appropriate sexual-health assessment may be required.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"surgical-treatment\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"surgical-treatment\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Surgical treatment\",\"heading\":\"Circumcision as a treatment for recurrent balanitis\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-3\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical rationale\",\"title\":\"Is recurrent balanitis a reason for circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eYes, significant recurrent balanitis can be a medical reason for circumcision, particularly where episodes repeatedly return and the foreskin itself is contributing to the problem.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision permanently removes the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor a patient whose recurrent balanitis is closely associated with a tight, scarred or repeatedly inflamed foreskin, removing that foreskin removes an important site contributing to the recurring problem.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe assess the penis and foreskin before deciding how the circumcision should be performed.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Long-term solution\",\"title\":\"Why can circumcision help recurrent balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThe space underneath the foreskin can retain moisture and secretions, particularly where the foreskin is tight and difficult to retract.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision removes the foreskin permanently and leaves the glans exposed.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis means there is no longer a foreskin covering the glans or a tight foreskin opening to retract for cleaning.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFor patients whose recurrent inflammation is related to their foreskin, this can provide a long-term surgical solution.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, circumcision does not prevent every possible inflammatory, dermatological or infectious condition that can affect the glans.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Realistic expectations\",\"title\":\"Does circumcision guarantee I will never get inflammation again?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eNo.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision removes the foreskin, so foreskin inflammation and foreskin-related tightness can no longer occur in the same way.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, the glans and surrounding penile skin remain present and can still be affected by dermatological conditions, irritation or infection.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe therefore describe circumcision as a definitive treatment for the foreskin component of recurrent balanitis rather than promising that a patient can never experience penile inflammation again.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"assessment\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"assessment\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical assessment\",\"heading\":\"Our approach to circumcision for recurrent balanitis\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We do not treat every patient with recurrent balanitis as though the foreskin is identical.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Before circumcision we examine:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether the foreskin retracts normally</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether there is a tight foreskin ring</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether scarring is present</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether there are white or abnormal areas suggesting lichen sclerosis / BXO</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether there is cracking or splitting</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether the frenulum is also tight or scarred</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether the penis is buried or retractile</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether there are other findings requiring medical assessment</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This helps us plan the circumcision according to the actual problem rather than using exactly the same approach for every patient.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Medical history\",\"title\":\"What happens at the assessment?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eWe first ask about the history of your symptoms.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIt is useful for us to know:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eHow often the balanitis occurs\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eHow long the problem has been happening\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhat symptoms you experience\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether your foreskin retracts normally\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether it has become tighter over time\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether the foreskin cracks or bleeds\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether erections are painful\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether you have been diagnosed with diabetes\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether there are white or scarred areas\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether you have had previous penile surgery\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWhether you have concerns about sexually transmitted infection\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eWe then examine the penis and foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis helps determine whether there is associated phimosis, scarring, lichen sclerosis / BXO, a frenulum problem or another finding that affects the circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Acute episodes\",\"title\":\"What if I currently have severe balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eIf the penis is acutely and significantly inflamed, infected or swollen when you contact us, tell us before attending for circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIt may not be appropriate to perform an elective circumcision during a severe acute episode.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou may first require appropriate medical assessment of the active inflammation.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eOnce the acute problem has settled sufficiently, circumcision can then be considered for patients experiencing recurrent episodes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"procedure\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"procedure\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Surgical procedure\",\"heading\":\"What happens during circumcision for recurrent balanitis?\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Adult technique\",\"title\":\"What happens during circumcision for recurrent balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eFor suitable adults at Beverley Clinic, circumcision is normally performed under local anaesthetic.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou remain awake during the procedure.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe commonly use a forceps-guided circumcision technique with thermal cautery.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe foreskin is carefully assessed and positioned so that the appropriate tissue can be removed. Thermal cautery helps control bleeding during the procedure.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eDepending on the individual wound, closure may involve dissolvable stitches, medical skin glue, a combination of both, or occasionally neither.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou receive detailed aftercare instructions before leaving the clinic.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/adults/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about adult circumcision →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Associated phimosis\",\"title\":\"What if I have phimosis as well as recurrent balanitis?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThis is a common combination.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhere a patient has both recurrent balanitis and phimosis, we pay particular attention to the tight or scarred area of foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe circumcision needs to adequately address the problematic tight foreskin rather than simply removing an arbitrary amount of skin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about circumcision for phimosis →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"BXO involvement\",\"title\":\"What if I have lichen sclerosis / BXO?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eIf the foreskin appears significantly white, scarred or affected by lichen sclerosis / BXO, this influences the planning of the circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe abnormal foreskin needs to be recognised so that the problematic tissue can be appropriately treated.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf the condition appears to involve areas beyond the foreskin, further medical follow-up may sometimes be required after circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/bxo/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about lichen sclerosis / BXO →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Tight frenulum\",\"title\":\"What if I have a tight frenulum as well?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThe frenulum is the band of tissue on the underside of the penis connecting the foreskin to the glans.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSome patients with foreskin problems also have a short, tight or scarred frenulum.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe assess this before circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf the frenulum is contributing to pain, tearing or pulling, we can discuss whether it should be treated as part of the procedure.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/procedures/frenuloplasty/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about tight frenulum →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"age-groups\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"age-groups\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"By age group\",\"heading\":\"Circumcision for recurrent balanitis across different ages\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Children \\u0026 teenagers\",\"title\":\"Circumcision for recurrent balanitis in children\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eChildren can also experience recurrent inflammation of the foreskin and glans.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIt is important to remember that a non-retractable foreskin can be completely normal in a young boy, so inability to retract alone does not mean that circumcision is medically required.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, repeated troublesome episodes of balanitis or balanoposthitis, particularly where there is pathological foreskin tightness or scarring, may be a reason for circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe assess the child's age, symptoms and foreskin before proceeding.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/children/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about circumcision for children →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Eighteen \\u0026 over\",\"title\":\"Circumcision for recurrent balanitis in adults\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eWe regularly assess adult patients with recurrent balanitis at Beverley Clinic in Edgware, North-West London.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSome have experienced episodes for many years. Others develop the problem later in life, sometimes together with increasing foreskin tightness.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe examine the foreskin to establish whether there is phimosis, scarring, lichen sclerosis / BXO or another foreskin problem relevant to the circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSuitable adults are normally circumcised under local anaesthetic.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/adults/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about adult circumcision →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"recovery\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"recovery\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Recovery \\u0026 aftercare\",\"heading\":\"Healing timeline and returning to daily activities\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Healing process\",\"title\":\"What is recovery like after circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eSome swelling, bruising, tenderness and increased sensitivity are expected during early healing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe glans will remain exposed following circumcision and may initially feel more sensitive, particularly if a tight foreskin previously meant that it was rarely exposed.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf dissolvable stitches are used, they gradually loosen as the wound heals.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe early appearance is not the final cosmetic result. Swelling settles and the circumcision scar continues changing during healing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou receive detailed written aftercare explaining how to care for the wound and when to contact us.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/aftercare/\\u0022\\u003eRead our circumcision aftercare information →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Employment\",\"title\":\"When can I return to work?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThis depends on the type of work you do and your individual recovery.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSomeone doing desk-based work may be able to return sooner than somebody whose job involves heavy lifting or significant physical activity.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe advise you according to the procedure and your circumstances.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Physical exercise\",\"title\":\"When can I exercise after circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eStrenuous exercise, cycling, heavy lifting and activities that cause significant movement or friction around the wound should be avoided during the early healing period.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eReturn to exercise gradually according to your healing and the instructions we give you.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Sexual activity\",\"title\":\"When can I have sex after circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eAt Beverley Clinic, we generally advise patients to avoid sexual intercourse and masturbation for at least two weeks after circumcision and until the wound is sufficiently healed.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHealing varies between patients.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf the wound has not healed sufficiently at two weeks, or sexual activity causes discomfort or places tension on the wound, you should wait longer.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eFollow the individual aftercare advice given to you following your procedure.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"risks-and-advice\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"risks-and-advice\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Safety \\u0026 advice\",\"heading\":\"Risks of surgery and when to seek medical advice\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Surgical risks\",\"title\":\"What are the risks of circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision is a surgical procedure and complications can occur.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003ePotential problems include:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBleeding\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eInfection\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSwelling\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eBruising\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eWound separation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eDelayed healing\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eScarring\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eAltered sensation\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eCosmetic dissatisfaction\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eNeed for further treatment\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eCircumcision permanently changes the appearance of the penis because the foreskin is removed and the glans remains exposed.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe explain the relevant risks before treatment so that you can make an informed decision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"When to seek advice\",\"title\":\"When should I seek medical advice rather than simply booking circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eBalanitis is usually not an emergency, but some symptoms require medical assessment.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eSeek appropriate medical advice if:\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cul\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThis is your first significant episode and the cause is unknown\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThe inflammation is severe\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou feel generally unwell\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou have difficulty passing urine\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThere is urethral discharge\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou have genital ulcers or an unusual lesion\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eYou are concerned about a sexually transmitted infection\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThere is a persistent area of abnormal skin\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eThere is unexplained bleeding\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003cli\\u003eSymptoms are persistent or worsening\\u003c/li\\u003e\\u003c/ul\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eIf you cannot pass urine at all or believe you have a medical emergency, seek urgent medical attention rather than waiting for a routine circumcision appointment.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"pricing\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"pricing\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Transparent pricing\",\"heading\":\"How much does circumcision for recurrent balanitis cost?\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Medical foreskin problem\",\"title\":\"How much does circumcision for recurrent balanitis cost?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eAt our London clinic, adult circumcision involving a medical or foreskin problem, with or without frenulum treatment, is currently £1,080.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eChildren with medical foreskin problems are assessed individually.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf you are unsure which price applies to you, contact us before booking.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/prices/\\u0022\\u003eView circumcision prices →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Edgware practice\",\"title\":\"Beverley Clinic in North-West London\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eOur clinic is based at 78 Beverley Drive, Edgware, HA8 5NE.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe provide circumcision services in a dedicated, CQC-registered clinical setting with experienced practitioners.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe regularly see patients with recurrent balanitis, phimosis, and foreskin concerns from across London and the UK.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"why-choose-us\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"why-choose-us\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Why choose us\",\"heading\":\"Why choose Beverley Clinic for circumcision for recurrent balanitis?\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-3\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical focus\",\"title\":\"Specialist focus on circumcision\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision is a core part of our day-to-day clinical work rather than an occasional procedure.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Underlying causes\",\"title\":\"We examine the underlying foreskin problem\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eRecurrent balanitis can occur alongside phimosis, scarring, lichen sclerosis / BXO or a tight frenulum.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWe assess these features before planning the circumcision.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Tailored treatment\",\"title\":\"Circumcision tailored to the patient\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eWe consider the patient's age, anatomy, symptoms and findings on examination rather than using exactly the same approach for everyone.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"All ages\",\"title\":\"Children, teenagers and adults\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eWe circumcise patients across different age groups and adapt the procedure and aftercare accordingly.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Follow-up care\",\"title\":\"Follow-up during healing\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003ePatients receive preparation information before treatment, detailed aftercare afterwards and follow-up support during healing.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/faq {\"heading\":\"Frequently asked questions about balanitis and circumcision\",\"items\":[{\"q\":\"What is balanitis?\",\"a\":\"Balanitis is inflammation of the head of the penis (glans). It can cause redness, swelling, soreness, itching and discomfort.\"},{\"q\":\"What is balanoposthitis?\",\"a\":\"Balanoposthitis means inflammation affecting both the glans and foreskin.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does my balanitis keep returning?\",\"a\":\"Recurrent balanitis can have several causes. A tight or difficult-to-retract foreskin, repeated irritation, infection, diabetes, scarring and lichen sclerosis / BXO can all be relevant.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a tight foreskin cause balanitis?\",\"a\":\"Yes. A tight foreskin can make retraction and cleaning difficult and may be associated with recurrent inflammation.\"},{\"q\":\"Can balanitis cause phimosis?\",\"a\":\"Repeated inflammation can contribute to scarring and increasing tightness of the foreskin in some patients.\"},{\"q\":\"Does balanitis mean I need circumcision?\",\"a\":\"Not necessarily. A single episode of balanitis does not automatically require circumcision. Circumcision is particularly relevant to patients with significant recurrent balanitis where the foreskin is contributing to the problem.\"},{\"q\":\"Can circumcision stop recurrent balanitis?\",\"a\":\"Circumcision removes the foreskin permanently and can provide a definitive treatment for the foreskin component of recurrent balanitis. It does not guarantee that the glans can never develop another inflammatory or dermatological condition.\"},{\"q\":\"Can balanitis be caused by diabetes?\",\"a\":\"Recurrent genital inflammation can be associated with diabetes, particularly where blood glucose is poorly controlled. Adults with recurrent balanitis may need appropriate medical assessment for underlying causes.\"},{\"q\":\"Is balanitis an STI?\",\"a\":\"Balanitis itself is not a specific sexually transmitted infection. However, some sexually transmitted conditions can cause similar symptoms, so appropriate sexual-health assessment may be required where there is a relevant risk.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between balanitis and phimosis?\",\"a\":\"Balanitis is inflammation of the glans. Phimosis is a foreskin that is too tight to retract. The two conditions can occur together.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between balanitis and BXO?\",\"a\":\"Balanitis describes inflammation of the glans. BXO is associated with lichen sclerosis and can cause characteristic chronic changes and scarring affecting the foreskin and penis.\"},{\"q\":\"Why has my foreskin become tighter after repeated balanitis?\",\"a\":\"Repeated inflammation and healing can sometimes contribute to loss of elasticity and scarring, causing the foreskin to become progressively tighter.\"},{\"q\":\"Can children get recurrent balanitis?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Children can experience balanitis and balanoposthitis. Repeated troublesome episodes may be a reason for circumcision after appropriate assessment.\"},{\"q\":\"Can adults be circumcised under local anaesthetic?\",\"a\":\"Suitable adults at our clinic are normally circumcised using local anaesthetic.\"},{\"q\":\"When can I have sex after circumcision?\",\"a\":\"Our clinic generally advises waiting at least two weeks and until the wound is sufficiently healed. If healing is incomplete at two weeks, wait longer and follow your individual aftercare instructions.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I speak to someone before booking?\",\"a\":\"Yes. If you have recurrent balanitis and are unsure whether your foreskin is contributing to the problem, contact us and explain your symptoms.\"}]} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"consultation\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"consultation\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Recurrent balanitis circumcision in London</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you experience repeated episodes of balanitis and are considering circumcision, contact Beverley Clinic.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We can assess the foreskin and determine whether there is associated phimosis, scarring, lichen sclerosis / BXO or another foreskin problem that needs to be considered when planning your circumcision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/book/\">Book a consultation →</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/callout {\"title\":\"If this is urgent, do not wait for an appointment\",\"body\":\"\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:10px\\u0022\\u003eGo to A\\u0026amp;E today if a pulled-back foreskin is trapped behind the head of the penis and swelling, if you or your child cannot pass urine, or if there is spreading redness with a fever. Those need treating in hours. For anything else, call \\u003ca href=\\u0022tel:+442089513794\\u0022 data-track=\\u0022call-urgent\\u0022 style=\\u0022color:var(\\u002d\\u002dblue-deep);font-weight:600\\u0022\\u003e020 8951 3794\\u003c/a\\u003e and tell us what is happening, and we will bring you forward if you need it.\\u003c/p\\u003e\",\"urgent\":true,\"level\":2} /--></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/callback-card {\"eyebrow\":\"Request a call back\",\"title\":\"Ask us first\",\"formId\":\"balanitis\",\"subject\":\"balanitis enquiry\",\"urgent\":true} /--></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/cta-band {\"title\":\"Book a consultation\",\"text\":\"You can request a consultation or book by contacting the clinic. Tell us about any medical conditions, medication, allergies or bleeding problems when you book.\",\"eyebrow\":\"Talk to us\",\"ctaLabel\":\"Book a consultation\",\"ctaUrl\":\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/book/\",\"phoneLabel\":\"Call 020 8951 3794\",\"phoneUrl\":\"tel:+442089513794\",\"cardTitle\":\"Would you rather write than speak?\",\"cardHtml\":\"\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:10px\\u0022\\u003eA phone call asks you to be fluent and composed in the moment. \\u003ca href=\\u0022https://wa.me/447886779958\\u0022 rel=\\u0022noopener\\u0022 target=\\u0022_blank\\u0022 data-track=\\u0022whatsapp-cta\\u0022 style=\\u0022color:var(\\u002d\\u002dblue-deep)\\u0022\\u003eWhatsApp\\u003c/a\\u003e does not. You can take your time, translate, and forward the answer to whoever else in the family needs to see it.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp style=\\u0022margin-top:12px\\u0022\\u003eWe reply during opening hours, and we are used to the questions people feel awkward asking out loud.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Also at this clinic\",\"heading\":\"Circumcision across all ages\",\"lede\":\"\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/group-cards {\"level\":3,\"banded\":false} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->"
}
